  "full_response": "When designing RESTful APIs, it's important to follow some best practices to ensure that your API is easy to use, maintainable, and successful. Here are some best practices to consider:\n\n1. Resource-oriented: RESTful APIs should be based on resources and use standard HTTP methods (GET, POST, PUT, DELETE) to manipulate those resources. Resources should have a unique URI (Uniform Resource Identifier) and should be exposed to clients in a consistent manner.\n\n2. Use HTTP status codes and error messages: When designing your API, make sure you use standard HTTP status codes (e.g., 200, 400, 404, 500) to indicate the success or failure of an operation. Additionally, provide meaningful error messages that help the caller understand what went wrong.\n\n3. Use consistent naming conventions: Choose a convention for naming your API resources, HTTP methods, and URIs. Consistent naming will make it easier for developers to understand and use your API.\n\n4. Support versioning: When modifying your API, it's important to support versioning. This will help prevent breaking changes for consumers of your API.\n\n5. Document your API: Create a comprehensive API documentation that covers all the available resources, their methods, and the expected request and response formats. This documentation will help your API consumers to understand and use your API.\n\n6. Secure your API: Work to secure your API by considering the following measures:\n\t* Use HTTPS to encrypt sensitive data.\n\t* Add authentication mechanisms such as OAuth or API keys to control access to your API.\n\t* Use rate limiting and retries to protect your from abuse.\n7. Reduce the number of HTTP requests: Minimize the amount of data transmitted by your API by utilizing techniques like pagination, filtering, and caching. This will help optimize your API's performance and reduce the client's latency.",
